35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"I found that the steady, descriptive projects often leave a lasting legacy, while the more ambitious ones sometimes leave their questions unanswered.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"My findings are concerning. There is a severe shortage of trained technical hands in the wet lab, though the computational side is saturated.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,I found that our shared instrumentation is fragile. A single engineer for three institutes is a critical failure point for any high-throughput project.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Fascinating. It seems the agency still absorbs computing costs, but the awardee is now burdened with rising animal-unit rates and shipping.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,I found that field-based work is incredibly vulnerable to environmental interruptions. Many projects are losing entire seasons to things they cannot control.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"It makes me wonder if we should favor the ""plainer"" projects that are more likely to deliver a complete result.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, your point about the engineer is critical. If the technical staff is gone and the machines are broken, the project is dead on arrival.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,Exactly. We cannot fund a proposal that relies on a facility that is already at a breaking point.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,Logic suggests we should prioritize projects that leverage the centrally funded computing resources rather than those requiring expensive animal facilities.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"I agree. If a project depends on a specific field site or a fragile breeding colony, the risk of non-scientific delay is just too high.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I feel for the ambitious researchers, but I believe it is kinder to fund a project that can actually succeed.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,I cannot support any proposal that requires specialized technicians who no longer exist in our current staffing rolls.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"I am observing a pattern. The ""high-impact"" projects are the ones most dependent on the very services that are currently failing.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a classic case of the ""ambition gap."" The grant size is static, but the cost of ambition has increased.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"I'm worried that if we ignore these bottlenecks, we're just setting the researcher up for a stressful failure.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
